WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.968 --> 00:00:02.133
-Thank you for being here.

00:00:02.133 --> 00:00:03.334
I appreciate you being here.

00:00:03.334 --> 00:00:05.000
Your family came with you
on this trip to New York.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.234
-Of course they did.
-Yeah, which I do love.

00:00:07.234 --> 00:00:08.734
You know who's stealing
the show, though?

00:00:08.734 --> 00:00:11.534
It's -- It's granny.
-It's Granzo Pat.

00:00:11.534 --> 00:00:13.701
-Granny Pat, I'm in love with.

00:00:13.701 --> 00:00:15.400
-Everyone is.
Everyone treats her

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:18.067
like a princess, and she's like,
"I'm here."

00:00:18.067 --> 00:00:19.334
[ Laughter ]

00:00:19.334 --> 00:00:20.601
-But -- does she --
Here's a picture of you

00:00:20.601 --> 00:00:22.100
and Granny on the red carpet.
-No!

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:24.767
-No, come on.
Yeah.

00:00:24.767 --> 00:00:26.501
[ Audience "Awws" ]
-Yeah.

00:00:26.501 --> 00:00:28.601
-How is she loving
her newfound fame?

00:00:28.601 --> 00:00:30.067
-You know what?

00:00:30.067 --> 00:00:32.601
She actually
got her first gifting.

00:00:32.601 --> 00:00:34.067
-First what?
-Gifting.

00:00:34.067 --> 00:00:37.033
Like, she's now so famous that
people are gifting her things.

00:00:37.033 --> 00:00:39.534
[ Laughter ]
And I went to Ireland,

00:00:39.534 --> 00:00:42.901
and someone came and gave her a
gift, and when she received it,

00:00:42.901 --> 00:00:46.234
months later, she goes,
"You must -- You must give me

00:00:46.234 --> 00:00:48.501
the address of that person."
[ Laughter ]

00:00:48.501 --> 00:00:50.667
And I was like, "Which person
are you talking about?"

00:00:50.667 --> 00:00:54.033
And she goes, "The person that
gave me that beautiful handbag.

00:00:54.033 --> 00:00:56.000
I need to write her
a thank-you note."

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:57.968
[ Audience "Awws" ]
-See, that's exactly right.

00:00:57.968 --> 00:00:59.467
-And I was like, "Granny,
I have no clue who that is."

00:00:59.467 --> 00:01:00.667
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:01:00.667 --> 00:01:02.634
No one even does that --
Good for Granny Pat.

00:01:02.634 --> 00:01:04.000
I love her.
-I know, but she's gonna

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:07.567
wear it, at some point, and show
that she's very thankful.

00:01:07.567 --> 00:01:09.267
-And how does she get, like --
does she --

00:01:09.267 --> 00:01:10.868
does she mingle
with other celebrities?

00:01:10.868 --> 00:01:12.567
-I don't know.
Oh, yeah, no, no, actually, no.

00:01:12.567 --> 00:01:15.868
One time, at Venice, she kept on
going up to Chris Pine and --

00:01:15.868 --> 00:01:17.667
[ Laughter ]

00:01:17.667 --> 00:01:19.267
She kept on going up
and being like,

00:01:19.267 --> 00:01:20.801
"I'm more famous than you."

00:01:20.801 --> 00:01:22.467
[ Laughter ]

00:01:22.467 --> 00:01:23.701
-Come on.
-I know.

00:01:23.701 --> 00:01:25.167
-That's the best.
-Yeah.

00:01:25.167 --> 00:01:28.167
-I saw you at the Oscars.
You were fantastic.

00:01:28.167 --> 00:01:29.801
Again, here's -- this is --

00:01:29.801 --> 00:01:31.534
this is what a movie star
looks like right there.

00:01:31.534 --> 00:01:32.934
-Aww.
-Come on.

00:01:32.934 --> 00:01:35.033
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:35.033 --> 00:01:37.167
Our buddy Andrew Garfield.

00:01:37.167 --> 00:01:39.067
And the Internet went wild,
by the way.

00:01:39.067 --> 00:01:41.667
They were like, "Those two
should do a movie together."

00:01:41.667 --> 00:01:43.400
-You know what's crazy is, we --

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:45.901
They didn't know that we were
doing a movie together.

00:01:45.901 --> 00:01:48.501
Well, I don't think they did,
but this was, I think,

00:01:48.501 --> 00:01:50.167
a bit of an accident.
-Yeah.

00:01:50.167 --> 00:01:51.767
-Which is even weirder --
-They just thought you guys

00:01:51.767 --> 00:01:52.901
would have good chemistry
together.

00:01:52.901 --> 00:01:54.234
-Yeah, they put us together,
and then...

00:01:54.234 --> 00:01:55.834
-You are doing a --
-We are doing a movie together.

00:01:55.834 --> 00:01:58.000
Don't worry. That's not a lie.
We are doing a movie together.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:00.968
-Yeah!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:00.968 --> 00:02:03.534
-And -- I --
I met him the day before

00:02:03.534 --> 00:02:06.100
in rehearsals,
and we instantly got along,

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:08.133
and I was takin' the piss out
of him, and he loved it.

00:02:08.133 --> 00:02:09.701
And he was takin' the piss out
of me, and I loved it.

00:02:09.701 --> 00:02:10.868
It was great.
-Yeah.

00:02:10.868 --> 00:02:12.200
That's what it's all about.

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:13.701
I want -- I want to talk about
your new movie, okay?

00:02:13.701 --> 00:02:14.901
"A Good Person."
-Yes.

00:02:14.901 --> 00:02:16.367
-This is written and directed
by Zach Braff.

00:02:16.367 --> 00:02:17.467
-Yes.
-Who I love.

00:02:17.467 --> 00:02:18.634
He's a great guy.

00:02:18.634 --> 00:02:20.133
So talented,
but also super funny.

00:02:20.133 --> 00:02:21.534
-Yeah, let's give him applause.

00:02:21.534 --> 00:02:23.267
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Yeah, love Zach Braff.

00:02:23.267 --> 00:02:25.634
I like that guy.
-Yes.

00:02:25.634 --> 00:02:28.367
He told me to have fun tonight,
and as he texted,

00:02:28.367 --> 00:02:31.300
"Tell mommy I love him."
[ Laughter ]

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:34.000
And it was a typo
and actually was "Jimmy."

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:35.033
[ Laughter ]
-I was gonna say, I was like,

00:02:35.033 --> 00:02:36.434
I don't know if he --
I was like,

00:02:36.434 --> 00:02:37.901
"Did he ever call me mommy?
That's cool."

00:02:37.901 --> 00:02:40.801
-But I love -- I love the fact
that "Tell mommy I love him."

00:02:40.801 --> 00:02:43.934
-Yeah, tell -- tell --
-Tell mommy you love him, too.

00:02:43.934 --> 00:02:45.434
-Tell him mommy loves him
so much.

00:02:45.434 --> 00:02:48.567
Mommy loves you, Zach!
[ Laughter ]

00:02:48.567 --> 00:02:50.567
But he --
he wrote this film for you.

00:02:50.567 --> 00:02:51.801
-He did.
He wrote it for me.

00:02:51.801 --> 00:02:53.734
-You were his muse. I mean --
-I was his muse.

00:02:53.734 --> 00:02:56.567
I think, also, there's like --
there's so much ease

00:02:56.567 --> 00:02:59.634
with reading a script when
someone knows how you talk

00:02:59.634 --> 00:03:02.334
and how far they can, like,
push you

00:03:02.334 --> 00:03:03.534
and what
you're capable of doing.

00:03:03.534 --> 00:03:04.834
So it was just a joy.
-Yeah.

00:03:04.834 --> 00:03:07.133
-It was so much fun.
-And I got to say, also,

00:03:07.133 --> 00:03:09.267
I love Molly Shannon.
Who doesn't love Molly Shannon?

00:03:09.267 --> 00:03:10.801
But she's in the film, as well.
-She is.

00:03:10.801 --> 00:03:12.567
She plays my mother.
Molly Shannon.

00:03:12.567 --> 00:03:14.767
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:14.767 --> 00:03:16.334
-How was Molly?
Just fun, right?

00:03:16.334 --> 00:03:18.000
-Unbelievable.
So funny.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:20.200
So amazing to work with.
We have this epic scene

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:22.767
where we, like -- we --
we chase each other --

00:03:22.767 --> 00:03:25.267
Well, I run away from her,
and she's chasing after me,

00:03:25.267 --> 00:03:27.868
and it gets into
quite a bit of a tussle.

00:03:27.868 --> 00:03:31.534
And halfway through fighting --
like, doing the scene,

00:03:31.534 --> 00:03:33.734
we had to pause because it was
getting quite aggressive,

00:03:33.734 --> 00:03:36.734
and instead of us, like,
maybe not fighting as hard,

00:03:36.734 --> 00:03:39.067
she was like,
"Let's put some pads on."

00:03:39.067 --> 00:03:40.267
[ Laughter ]
And that --

00:03:40.267 --> 00:03:41.734
that was the approach.
It was like,

00:03:41.734 --> 00:03:44.300
"Instead of not actually
beating each other up,

00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:46.033
let's put pads on
so we can go harder."

00:03:46.033 --> 00:03:47.367
-Really go harder.
-Yeah.

00:03:47.367 --> 00:03:48.767
[ Laughter ]

00:03:48.767 --> 00:03:50.300
-That is so Molly, right?
-Yeah.

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:52.734
-That's perfect.
You also get to --

00:03:52.734 --> 00:03:54.400
You wrote and you get to sing
in this movie.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:55.400
-I do, yeah.

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:56.834
-And what a lovely voice
you have.

00:03:56.834 --> 00:03:58.033
And it's --
-Thank you.

00:03:58.033 --> 00:03:59.167
[ Cheers and applause ]
Thank you.

00:03:59.167 --> 00:04:00.667
-I want to --
I'd love to see more.

00:04:00.667 --> 00:04:02.667
I want to hear more.
-Well, what's cool is --

00:04:02.667 --> 00:04:04.868
is it's the first time
I've actually written a song

00:04:04.868 --> 00:04:08.367
for a character and performed a
song as the character, as well.

00:04:08.367 --> 00:04:11.100
So that was really nice.
But then outside of the movie,

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:13.934
I got to record them
as how I would like them, as me,

00:04:13.934 --> 00:04:17.434
as, like, the creator.
So when the movie comes out,

00:04:17.434 --> 00:04:19.467
my songs are gonna be
on the soundtrack, as well.

00:04:19.467 --> 00:04:22.067
So have a little --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:22.067 --> 00:04:23.868
-Do you get --
Do you get nervous?

00:04:23.868 --> 00:04:26.434
'Cause, obviously, we know you
can act, but you're like --

00:04:26.434 --> 00:04:27.901
Singing is kind of
a private thing you do,

00:04:27.901 --> 00:04:29.367
like, in your house.
And you're -- I'm not sure --

00:04:29.367 --> 00:04:30.801
in front of people.
-Yeah.

00:04:30.801 --> 00:04:32.767
I think -- well, for years,
I was always always more

00:04:32.767 --> 00:04:34.167
of a singer-songwriter.

00:04:34.167 --> 00:04:36.167
It was happening more when I was
younger, singer-songwriting,

00:04:36.167 --> 00:04:39.601
than it was acting.
And then when I did first gig

00:04:39.601 --> 00:04:41.968
as an actor,
it just took off in that way.

00:04:41.968 --> 00:04:43.400
And so I hadn't done it
in a long while.

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:45.067
So, yeah, there's a bit
of confidence-building,

00:04:45.067 --> 00:04:46.601
but I'm really excited to do it.

00:04:46.601 --> 00:04:47.767
I've been wanting to do it
for ages.

00:04:47.767 --> 00:04:49.834
-Yeah, oh, crusher.
That's great.

00:04:49.834 --> 00:04:53.067
In the movie, your character
cuts her own hair.

00:04:53.067 --> 00:04:55.067
-Yeah.
-And I heard that you

00:04:55.067 --> 00:04:56.801
actually said,
"I'll cut my own hair."

00:04:56.801 --> 00:04:59.634
-It was my idea.
I was like, "Zach, I think

00:04:59.634 --> 00:05:01.501
I should chop my hair off
at the beginning of the movie."

00:05:01.501 --> 00:05:03.033
And he was like...
-The beginning of the movie.

00:05:03.033 --> 00:05:04.501
-..."Cool.
That's not gonna work."

00:05:04.501 --> 00:05:05.901
-Yeah, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:05.901 --> 00:05:09.033
"We have the whole movie shoot."
-Yeah, initially, it was that --

00:05:09.033 --> 00:05:11.367
it was -- but how do we
do the shooting in order?

00:05:11.367 --> 00:05:13.300
And I was like,
"You'll figure it out."

00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:14.901
[ Laughter ]
-"You'll figure it out?"

00:05:14.901 --> 00:05:16.434
-Yeah, and then
they got together,

00:05:16.434 --> 00:05:17.934
and they all had
big conversations.

00:05:17.934 --> 00:05:19.968
And they were like,
"Zach, this is insane.

00:05:19.968 --> 00:05:23.467
She can't do this.
This is not helpful at all."

00:05:23.467 --> 00:05:25.934
So he came and spoke to me,
very, like, tentatively

00:05:25.934 --> 00:05:28.534
and was like, "Florence,
we can't do this.

00:05:28.534 --> 00:05:30.133
We're gonna need to use a wig."
-"We'll use a wig."

00:05:30.133 --> 00:05:31.667
-"You'll figure it out.
-Yeah.

00:05:31.667 --> 00:05:33.701
[ Laughter ]
-And they figured it out!

00:05:33.701 --> 00:05:35.033
-Yeah, you said, "You're gonna
have to figure it out,"

00:05:35.033 --> 00:05:36.534
'cause you did it.
-I did it!

00:05:36.534 --> 00:05:38.234
-You cut your own hair.
-That was --

00:05:38.234 --> 00:05:40.133
[ Laughing ]

00:05:40.133 --> 00:05:42.734
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:42.734 --> 00:05:44.701
So...
[ Both laugh ]

00:05:44.701 --> 00:05:47.300
-Yeah, beautiful job.

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:49.300
-So the point is, it's supposed
to be a bit shabby,

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:52.234
but, obviously, in between,
like, we were shooting,

00:05:52.234 --> 00:05:55.701
we were trying to shoot all
of it in -- in the one take.

00:05:55.701 --> 00:05:57.534
We couldn't --
we had to change the setup.

00:05:57.534 --> 00:05:58.934
-Yeah.
-And so there was one portion

00:05:58.934 --> 00:06:02.200
for about half an hour where I
had one really long strand

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:03.801
of hair down to my hip,

00:06:03.801 --> 00:06:05.567
and then the rest of it was
this short, like a unicorn.

00:06:05.567 --> 00:06:06.634
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:06.634 --> 00:06:08.534
-And it's like
the ultimate mullet.

00:06:08.534 --> 00:06:09.968
-And --
[ Laughter ]

00:06:09.968 --> 00:06:11.767
Can we say what
the movie's about to kind of

00:06:11.767 --> 00:06:12.834
explains why you would
cut your own hair?

00:06:12.834 --> 00:06:15.033
-Yes, why I'm doing that.
Sorry, guys.

00:06:15.033 --> 00:06:17.767
It is about --

00:06:17.767 --> 00:06:20.033
It's about addiction.
It's about grief.

00:06:20.033 --> 00:06:24.100
It's about guilt.
It's about the opioid crisis.

00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:29.133
And in fantastic Zach Braff
fashion, he finds

00:06:29.133 --> 00:06:32.100
moments of lightness in,

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:34.000
you know,
many stories of darkness.

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:38.300
And so we've created a movie
that hopefully will affect

00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:41.400
the right people and maybe some
others, too, along the way.

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:45.968
But, yeah, we find my character,
Allison, on this really

00:06:45.968 --> 00:06:50.834
tricky and tough journey about
recovery and about addiction.

00:06:50.834 --> 00:06:52.734
And I get to hang out with
Morgan Freeman

00:06:52.734 --> 00:06:54.367
and Molly Shannon.
-Yeah.

00:06:54.367 --> 00:06:55.834
-Yeah, ooh!
-It's really great.

00:06:55.834 --> 00:06:57.901
[ Cheers and applause ]
I want to show a clip.

00:06:57.901 --> 00:07:00.467
Here's Florence Pugh
in "A Good Person."

00:07:00.467 --> 00:07:01.934
Take a look at this.

00:07:01.934 --> 00:07:06.300
♪♪

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:07.367
-Hi.

00:07:07.367 --> 00:07:09.868
I'm here to get
a prescription refilled.

00:07:09.868 --> 00:07:11.200
Last name, Johnson.

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:12.968
Birthday, August 24th.

00:07:12.968 --> 00:07:14.801
-Okay.
For which medication?

00:07:14.801 --> 00:07:17.834
-Uh, the oxy.
Oxycontin.

00:07:17.834 --> 00:07:20.000
-It says here
you are out of refills.

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:22.601
I see you've been on them
for over a year now.

00:07:22.601 --> 00:07:24.601
Your doctor is probably trying
to wean you off of them.

00:07:24.601 --> 00:07:27.200
As I'm sure you know,
they can be very addictive.

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:29.300
-Oh, really?
I hadn't [bleep] heard that.

00:07:31.033 --> 00:07:32.767
Sorry.
I'm sorry.

00:07:32.767 --> 00:07:33.767
I'm sorry.

00:07:33.767 --> 00:07:35.367
-Yeah.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:35.367 --> 00:07:38.000
Florence Pugh, everyone!
You got to see it.

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:40.601
"A Good Person"
is in theaters this Friday.

00:07:40.601 --> 00:07:42.467
Florence and I are playing
a fun game when we come back.

00:07:42.467 --> 00:07:43.968
Stick around, everybody.

